### What is StampedLock?

StampedLock, introduced in Java 8, extends the concept of read-write locking with an optimistic read mode that avoids acquiring any lock at all. It provides three access modes, each returning a stamp that must be used to unlock or validate.

### How do the three modes work?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.StampedLock;

public class StampedLockDemo {
    private final StampedLock sl = new StampedLock();
    private BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");
    private BigDecimal interestRate = new BigDecimal("7.5");

    // MODE 1: Write lock (exclusive)
    public void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        long stamp = sl.writeLock();
        try {
            balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        } finally {
            sl.unlockWrite(stamp);
        }
    }

    // MODE 2: Read lock (shared, blocks writers)
    public BigDecimal getBalanceLocked() {
        long stamp = sl.readLock();
        try {
            return balance;
        } finally {
            sl.unlockRead(stamp);
        }
    }

    // MODE 3: Optimistic read (NO LOCK!)
    public BigDecimal getBalanceOptimistic() {
        long stamp = sl.tryOptimisticRead();   // get stamp, NO lock
        BigDecimal localBalance = balance;      // copy to local variable
        BigDecimal localRate = interestRate;     // copy to local variable
        if (sl.validate(stamp)) {               // any write since stamp?
            return localBalance;                 // NO write: data valid!
        }
        // Write happened: fall back to read lock
        stamp = sl.readLock();
        try {
            return balance;
        } finally {
            sl.unlockRead(stamp);
        }
    }
}
```

### How is StampedLock different from ReadWriteLock?

| Feature | StampedLock | ReentrantReadWriteLock |
|---|---|---|
| Optimistic read | Yes (zero lock overhead) | No |
| Read lock CAS | No (volatile read only) | Yes (CAS on reader count) |
| Reentrant | No (deadlock on re-lock!) | Yes |
| Condition support | No | Yes (on writeLock) |
| Fairness option | No | Yes |
| Lock downgrade | Yes (write to read) | Yes |
| Lock upgrade | Yes (read to write via tryConvertToWriteLock) | No |
| Best for | 99:1 read:write ratio | 95:5 read:write ratio |

### Why is optimistic read faster?

ReadWriteLock's readLock() must atomically increment a shared reader count using CAS. Under 100 concurrent reader threads, all 100 threads CAS the same shared counter, causing cache-line ping-pong and contention. StampedLock's tryOptimisticRead() is a single volatile read of the internal stamp (a long value that changes only when a write lock is acquired or released). Volatile reads do not modify shared state, so there is zero contention even with 1000 concurrent readers.

### What are the rules for optimistic read?

You must copy all shared data to local variables before calling validate(). You cannot read shared data after validate() because a write could happen between validate() returning true and the subsequent read.

```java
// WRONG: read after validate
long stamp = sl.tryOptimisticRead();
if (sl.validate(stamp)) {
    return balance;  // WRONG: write may happen between validate and return!
}

// CORRECT: copy before validate
long stamp = sl.tryOptimisticRead();
BigDecimal local = balance;    // copy first
if (sl.validate(stamp)) {
    return local;              // use the copy
}
```

### What are the common mistakes?

The most dangerous mistake is re-locking from the same thread. StampedLock is NOT reentrant. If a thread holds a read lock and tries to acquire a write lock (or vice versa), it deadlocks against itself.

```java
// WRONG: re-locking causes DEADLOCK
long stamp = sl.readLock();
try {
    long writeStamp = sl.writeLock();  // DEADLOCK! Same thread, not reentrant
} finally {
    sl.unlockRead(stamp);
}

// CORRECT: use tryConvertToWriteLock for upgrade
long stamp = sl.readLock();
try {
    long writeStamp = sl.tryConvertToWriteLock(stamp);
    if (writeStamp != 0L) {
        stamp = writeStamp;  // upgraded!
        balance = balance.add(interest);
    } else {
        sl.unlockRead(stamp);
        stamp = sl.writeLock();  // full write lock
        balance = balance.add(interest);
    }
} finally {
    sl.unlock(stamp);
}
```

### When should you use StampedLock?

Use StampedLock when reads vastly outnumber writes (99:1 or higher) and reads are short. The optimistic path handles the common case with zero overhead, and the rare write invalidation triggers a fallback to a full read lock.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can StampedLock replace ReentrantLock? No, because StampedLock is not reentrant and does not support Conditions.
- Is the optimistic stamp thread-safe? The stamp is a long value local to the reading thread. It does not need to be shared.
